// In Power Query, per column (illustrative M-language step names, not written by the student):
// Changed Type: DateKey -> Date
// Changed Type: RiderID, RouteID, CustomerID, DeliveryID -> Text
// Changed Type: PromisedMinutes, ActualMinutes -> Whole Number
// Changed Type: DeliveryFee -> Fixed decimal number
// Test: does RiderID + RiderID mean anything? No -> Text, not a number.

// Power Query steps, in order, as they appear in Applied Steps:
// Source -> Promoted Headers -> Changed Type -> Trimmed Text -> Filtered Rows
// Filter condition written as a rule, not a fact about one file:
// Table.SelectRows(#"Trimmed Text", each [CustomerID] <> null and [CustomerID] <> "")

// Flat table (wrong design): one row per delivery, RiderName repeated per delivery
// Card visual: Count(RiderName) = 41280   <- counts delivery-rows, not riders
// Correct question: "how many riders do we have" needs a table with grain
// one-row-per-rider (Count(RiderName) on the riders dimension table = 68)

// Model view: drag riders[RiderID] onto swiftroute_deliveries[RiderID]
// Relationship dialog should read: Cardinality = One to many (one on riders)
// Repeat for routes[RouteID], customers_business[CustomerID], dates[DateKey]
// Table tools -> Mark as date table -> choose DateKey column -> OK

// Diagnosis: relationship builds, but a card counting matched rows shows blanks
// for some values -> inspect the key column in Power Query, e.g. routes_raw
// RouteID column shows "LAG-07" and "LAG-07 " (trailing space) as different rows
// Fix (Power Query, both tables' key columns): Transform -> Format -> Trim
// Relationship properties: Cross filter direction = Single (default), not Both

Total Deliveries = COUNTROWS ( swiftroute_deliveries )
Total Revenue = SUM ( swiftroute_deliveries[DeliveryFee] )
// Verified values at the unfiltered total: Total Deliveries = 41280,
// Total Revenue = ₦247,680,000

On-Time Deliveries =
CALCULATE ( [Total Deliveries], swiftroute_deliveries[Status] = "On time" )

On-Time Rate = DIVIDE ( [On-Time Deliveries], [Total Deliveries] )
// Verified: On-Time Rate = 82.0% at the unfiltered total
// A rider with 0 deliveries filtered in: DIVIDE returns BLANK, not an error or 0

On-Time Deliveries =
CALCULATE ( [Total Deliveries], swiftroute_deliveries[Status] = "On time" )
// Read aloud: "work out Total Deliveries, but first, on top of whatever
// the visual already filtered, also require Status = 'On time'."

Deliveries Previous Month =
CALCULATE ( [Total Deliveries], DATEADD ( dates[DateKey], -1, MONTH ) )

Month on Month Change =
DIVIDE ( [Total Deliveries] - [Deliveries Previous Month], [Deliveries Previous Month] )
// Requires: dates table marked as the date table on DateKey (Topic 7.2)
// January's Deliveries Previous Month correctly returns BLANK - no December
// exists in this model, which is correct, not a bug.

// Verification workflow for On-Time Rate:
// 1. Predict: "near 82%, because the SLA is 90% and it is a known concern"
// 2. Run at total level: card shows 82.0%
// 3. Reconcile: switch to Table view, filter Status = "On time", read row
//    count directly (33,850) and divide by Total Deliveries (41,280) by hand
// 4. Break: filter to one low-volume rider, confirm no error, sensible value

Last Refreshed = "Data as at " & FORMAT ( MAX ( dates[DateKey] ), "dd MMM yyyy" )
// With Pro licence:    File -> Publish -> select workspace -> open link in browser
// Without Pro licence:  File -> Export -> PDF   (static, but real and submittable)

// Faulty (hardcoded denominator):
On-Time Rate = DIVIDE ( [On-Time Deliveries], 41280 )
// Fix - denominator references the base measure, so it moves with any filter:
On-Time Rate = DIVIDE ( [On-Time Deliveries], [Total Deliveries] )

// Faulty (numerator = denominator, always 100%):
Late Rate = DIVIDE (
    CALCULATE ( [Total Deliveries], swiftroute_deliveries[Status] = "Late" ),
    CALCULATE ( [Total Deliveries], swiftroute_deliveries[Status] = "Late" )
)
// Fix - denominator must be the unfiltered total:
Late Rate = DIVIDE (
    CALCULATE ( [Total Deliveries], swiftroute_deliveries[Status] = "Late" ),
    [Total Deliveries]
)
